      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Hi Mark, \n\ns/k/c/ ;-)\n\n\u003e I can see a couple of issues with this:\n\u003e \n\u003e you seem to lose the width of the access as you log it (HW usually *is* width-sensitive)\n\u003e \u003cPrasd\u003e I have not changed HW read/write operation and just logging after read and before write. \n\u003e \n\nIt is the logging I\u0027m referring to. You log a byte access the same as a 32bit access, and yet they are very different operations. If this is supposed to help debugging, that\u0027s not great.\n\n\u003e there is no way to select the scope of the tracing (it is very unlikely that you want to trace every single MMIO access in the system, and more likely that you\u0027d want a specific subsystem instead)\n\u003e \u003cPrasad\u003e Yes. You are right. We want every single MMIO access operation.\n\nReally? Across the whole system?\n\n\u003e this creates a new ABI, which has to be agreed upon upstream first\n\u003e \u003cPrasad\u003e I have tried upstreaming and but didn\u0027t land into upstream.\n\nWell, that\u0027s probably a good indication that the way you approach the problem isn\u0027t ideal. And I cannot imagine we want to take a new tracing ABI on our own.\n\n\u003e \n\u003e If you are trying to track accesses to devices that are not clock, it feels like you should involve the clock subsystem directly, and track the dependency between a device and its clock provider, rather than having a blanket MMIO tracing.\n\u003e \u003cPrasad\u003e Hi Mark, Qualcomm have this use case to identify the unblocked access through logging. There is no other way SW developer can identify all register reads and writes to debug their drivers in regular development and testing phase.\n\u003e \n\nPeople have been able to debug such issues for a couple of decades without this, by instrumenting the *specific* register accesses instead of a kernel wide \"dump everything\". What changed?\n\n\u003e Qualcomm uses this logging in the development phase and this tracevents are not enabled in commercial builds.\n\u003e \n\nThat\u0027s not the point. This creates an ABI that deviates from upstream, and I simply question the validity of the proposed use case.\n\nThanks,\n\n        M.",
